1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
They're hot.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
They're cool.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Souped up, low down.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
And ready to put on a show.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,000
These products of backyard engineering have changed the way we dream and drive.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, Custom Cars on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,420
Torque converter locks up a little, slows it back down.

8
00:00:41,421 --> 00:00:44,680
Oh, I didn't see those since the 60s.

9
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,060
They're a triumph of mechanical ingenuity.

10
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:52,220
And a visual feast on wheels.

11
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:58,000
From high boy roadsters, to hopping low riders,

12
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:01,140
to tricked-out import tuners.

13
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:07,400
Custom cars are as various as the people who craft them

14
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:09,740
and as singular as a fingerprint.

15
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:13,540
Cars are what we all have in common,

16
00:01:13,541 --> 00:01:18,680
but hot riders and customizers embrace them in a way that nobody else does.

17
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,600
They use their cars not only as transportation,

18
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:27,480
but as a means of creating something almost from scratch

19
00:01:27,481 --> 00:01:29,260
that they could call their own.

20
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:35,460
The classic customs and hot rods from decades past

21
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:36,740
are cherished by collectors

22
00:01:36,741 --> 00:01:40,020
and showcased in galleries and museums.

23
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:42,800
The Knee Camp Roaster was the first

24
00:01:42,801 --> 00:01:45,200
America's Most Beautiful Roaster winner,

25
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:47,660
1950, Oakland Roaster Show.

26
00:01:47,661 --> 00:01:49,900
If we ever put that cart for sale,

27
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:54,240
I think it would bring $400,000 or $500,000.

28
00:01:54,241 --> 00:02:02,460
But for most customizers, the point isn't to create a museum piece or even a prize for best in show.

29
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:06,460
It's to move on down the road to see and be seen.

30
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:15,480
One of the commonalities between hot rods, low riders, tuners, is that they can be built piece by piece by piece by piece.

31
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:19,900
These cars are like oysters, you know, one layer after another.

32
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:23,400
The car represents an art form.

33
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:31,000
The only difference between us and people that put it on paper is we do our art and then we drive it around.

34
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:33,380
That's what we do.

35
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:43,620
Whatever its vintage, restructuring an automobile to enhance its style is hard, physical work.

36
00:02:43,621 --> 00:02:50,220
Customizer Gene Winfield's masterful metalwork and exquisite paint schemes have made him a legend in the field.

37
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,380
At his shop in the Mojave Desert, he incorporates original designs

38
00:02:55,381 --> 00:03:04,280
from molded grills to louvered hoods to ensure that each car he customizes is unique.

39
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,840
Each and every car I look at it and maybe I study it for a little while

40
00:03:08,841 --> 00:03:12,800
and then I try to figure out things that I can do that will be a little different.

41
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:23,078
With custom cars, we try to put different grills, different taillights and things so that each and every car has at least some things that are totally different than

42
00:03:23,079 --> 00:03:23,880
the other guy.

43
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:30,840
Winfield lowers the roof of a 1949 Mercury the way he's done it for the last 50 years.

44
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:32,900
Drop an inch here.

45
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:40,540
With hand tools, elbow grease, and a hard-earned degree in backyard technology, it's called chopping the top.

46
00:03:40,541 --> 00:03:48,480
What we're aiming for here, of course, is a typical chopped mercury, which is, you know, all mercury should be chopped, in my opinion.

47
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:56,120
So that's what we're going to do, and we're going to chop it approximately four inches in front and five in the rear.

48
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:02,980
Winfield begins by installing temporary braces inside the car to keep the body stable.

49
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:09,640
Then he measures the windshield and calculates how much he'll remove from the supporting pillars to achieve the desired slant.

50
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:15,980
This is called the A pillar, this is the B pillar, and this is the C pillar.

51
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:21,740
So on this particular chop, I'm going to cut it out completely, I'm going to reangle it,

52
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:24,160
and then we're going to have what we call a slanted post.

53
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:30,260
While tracing his cuts along the belt line, Winfield marks a section for removal at the C pillar,

54
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:33,360
so the top will have room to settle when it's lowered.

55
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:36,700
Okay, so now we're going to start cutting.

56
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:42,540
Okay.

57
00:04:42,541 --> 00:04:56,541
After cutting the pillars, Winfield marks more cuts to flatten the curve of the roof.

58
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:01,540
See that's the big problem in the mercuries.

59
00:05:01,541 --> 00:05:04,540
They have a kind of a big dome in the roof right here.

60
00:05:04,541 --> 00:05:07,540
And we have to stretch that forward and get rid of that.

61
00:05:07,541 --> 00:05:11,460
allows the top to go forward and down to a new angle.

62
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:22,760
Finally, it's time to check out the chop.

63
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:33,600
The initial chop looks good, but Winfield will fine-tune its shape to achieve the ideal proportion.

64
00:05:33,601 --> 00:05:37,720
That's the most important thing on a Mercury is to get that flow.

65
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,760
And that's just super important to get that right.

66
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:44,380
That's the way we do it to get a classic chopped Mercury.

67
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:52,800
Custom work by Winfield and countless others is displayed at shows throughout the country.

68
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:56,440
See, notice how the steering is where you can put the box right here.

69
00:05:56,441 --> 00:06:01,420
There, drivers swap parts and stories and admire the details of each other's craft.

70
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:03,460
This is slick.

71
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:05,200
Oh, it's total Americana.

72
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:10,280
You're making a statement about who you are, what you do, what you enjoy,

73
00:06:10,281 --> 00:06:14,820
and you're letting everybody else see it and letting everybody else appreciate it.

74
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:22,180
For all their differences, car customizers have long shared a do-it-yourself spirit

75
00:06:22,181 --> 00:06:25,880
and a distinctly populist ethic.

76
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:32,500
For generations, they've been disregarding Detroit's top-of-the-line models

77
00:06:32,501 --> 00:06:34,580
in favor of everyday people's cars.

78
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:37,680
From Model As to modern imports.

79
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:41,760
And then doing whatever it takes to restyle them

80
00:06:41,761 --> 00:06:43,860
into the rods and customs of their dreams.

81
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:48,860
As far as the culture and the lifestyle, they're different.

82
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:50,220
The cars are very much different.

83
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:52,820
But the common factor is the passion.

84
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:01,620
Ever since cars began to roll off assembly lines,

85
00:07:01,621 --> 00:07:05,160
customizers have tried to make them look better and run faster.

86
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:09,620
But the root of today's custom cultures can be traced to the 1930s

87
00:07:09,621 --> 00:07:14,820
and a racing scene that took shape on the dry lakes of Southern California.

88
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:22,540
The dry lakes were just like, you know, a cemented lake.

89
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:24,120
I mean, it's perfectly flat.

90
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,480
It's so smooth and it's so hard,

91
00:07:26,620 --> 00:07:29,980
and you can just drive as fast as you want for as long as you want.

92
00:07:29,981 --> 00:07:31,660
And that's what these guys did.

93
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:36,160
By the mid-30s, the car of choice was a Ford Roadster.

94
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:43,080
The 32 Roadster was the first that came equipped with an inexpensive eight-cylinder engine, known as the Ford Flathead.

95
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:48,340
The car's distinctive grille, molded into the body, was undeniably stylish.

96
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:54,040
More practically, like other Roadsters, its cloth top and windshields were easily removed.

97
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:58,440
My father's 32 Roadster, that was the family car.

98
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:00,080
They only had one car.

99
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:01,480
I mean, they went to get the groceries.

100
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:04,300
They went to take my dad to work and back,

101
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:07,440
and then my mother used it when he wasn't using it.

102
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:11,620
And then come the weekend that they were going to go to the dry legs,

103
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:14,340
you know, the fenders came off, the running board came off, headlights.

104
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:18,440
The Roadster was the cheapest model in the line.

105
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:21,640
It was the lightest model in the line because it didn't have a roof.

106
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:23,360
It didn't have side windows.

107
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:25,060
And it was kind of sporty.

108
00:08:25,061 --> 00:08:32,660
With little history to guide them, the Dry Lakes racers customized their cars by trial and error.

109
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:37,340
Bigger tires in the rear changed the gear ratio for a better top speed,

110
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,380
while smaller ones up front decreased wind resistance.

111
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:44,180
The front end could be lowered by heating and dropping the front axle.

112
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:49,840
The result? A gently sloping angle, or rake, of 3 to 4 degrees,

113
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:53,500
which came to define the classic hot rod look for decades to come.

114
00:08:53,501 --> 00:08:57,540
When you race on the Dry Lakes, you can either make horsepower or you can streamline it.

115
00:08:57,580 --> 00:08:59,980
They both have the same effect. They both make the cargo faster.

116
00:09:00,520 --> 00:09:01,260
And they did both.

117
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:05,620
Many of the Dry Lakes racers were mechanical mavericks.

118
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:08,342
In the late 1930s, Vic Edelbrock Sr.

119
00:09:08,343 --> 00:09:12,040
designed and machined a manifold to increase air fuel intake.

120
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:15,820
He called it the slingshot because of its Y-shaped design.

121
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:21,980
Ed Iskandarian ground camshafts that increased engine performance at high RPMs.

122
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:28,180
It's guys that are smart enough to figure out how to make the stuff they need when they don't have it.

123
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:32,800
You didn't go buy your parts, you just took the existing parts and made them better,

124
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,840
or made a manifold like my dad did.

125
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:39,500
He had a knack of looking at something in an engine, an internal combustion engine,

126
00:09:39,501 --> 00:09:43,720
and figuring out what was wrong, what it needed, and to go from there.

127
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:50,380
The Dry Lake scene came to a temporary halt with the onset of World War II.

128
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:59,440
But many of the racers found work and developed new ideas in the wartime aeronautics industry.

129
00:09:59,441 --> 00:10:03,960
They came back from the war. They knew how to machine parts.

130
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:07,680
They knew what a combustion engine was like. They knew how superchargers worked.

131
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:12,500
They had some rudimentary idea about aerodynamics, witnessed the belly tank racers.

132
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:20,400
After the war, Dry Lakes racer Bill Burke built a race car using an aluminum fuel tank from a fighter plane.

133
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:29,180
He built a model for Alex Exidius, founder of the SoCal Speed Shop, who sat it on a pair of Ford Model A and V axles.

134
00:10:29,720 --> 00:10:38,940
Racing in Utah at Bonneville in 1952, Exidius' belly tank racer set five speed records and eventually exceeded 200 miles per hour.

135
00:10:38,941 --> 00:10:43,300
This is the car that put the SoCal Speed Shop and SoCal, California on the map.

136
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:49,520
This is Alex's original belly tank, which he began racing in 1948 through 1952.

137
00:10:49,521 --> 00:10:55,220
set a ton of records in this car with no roll cage, no safety belt, nothing.

138
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:03,340
A lot of young guys came back and they'd been flying P-51s and doing fighter jockeys

139
00:11:03,341 --> 00:11:06,220
and they were suddenly dumped back into slow motion.

140
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:11,000
And I think the need for speed was even greater in the post-war era.

141
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:12,980
And that's when it really took off.

142
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:16,720
As Dry Lake Racing grew more popular,

143
00:11:16,721 --> 00:11:20,000
its innovators began to set up shop throughout Southern California,

144
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:23,720
laying the basis for a growing industry in aftermarket parts.

145
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:27,060
As word spread, their products were snapped up

146
00:11:27,061 --> 00:11:29,220
by a very different group of car customizers,

147
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:32,480
Southern Moonshiners.

148
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:38,160
So they started mail-ordering camshafts and intake manifolds

149
00:11:38,161 --> 00:11:39,820
from their V8 flathead Fords

150
00:11:39,821 --> 00:11:43,780
to put into their 44 tankers or moonshine cars.

151
00:11:43,781 --> 00:11:49,840
When Junior Johnson would make his nightly run, and he was running White Lightning,

152
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:54,260
he had an old pickup truck, looked old on the outside, was all new on the inside,

153
00:11:54,580 --> 00:11:59,460
and it had an Edelbrock-equipped Ford flathead in it that absolutely ran away from the feds.

154
00:12:01,420 --> 00:12:06,400
Unlike the Dry Lakes racers, bootleggers needed to keep their cars looking stock on the outside

155
00:12:06,401 --> 00:12:08,520
so as not to attract lawful attention.

156
00:12:08,521 --> 00:12:15,520
1940 Ford business coupes were popular cars of choice.

157
00:12:15,521 --> 00:12:21,520
Along with engine modifications, back panels were removed to make room for contraband.

158
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:26,520
Leaf spring suspensions were fortified by adding steel leaves.

159
00:12:26,521 --> 00:12:32,520
Wider wheels and tires helped drivers navigate hairpin turns on bumpy back roads.

160
00:12:32,521 --> 00:12:35,520
Some drivers installed large stainless steel containers in the trunk,

161
00:12:35,521 --> 00:12:39,500
which were fitted with a valve that could release its contents in the midst of a chase.

162
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:45,680
After their Saturday night runs, the drivers often got together to race on Sundays,

163
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:51,300
competing for a few extra dollars on a burgeoning circuit that would come to be known as NASCAR.

164
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:55,160
That's how NASCAR started, and that's what NASCAR is today.

165
00:12:55,161 --> 00:13:01,100
It was the moonshiners running against each other with their stock-looking cars that really weren't stock underneath.

166
00:13:01,101 --> 00:13:06,460
Car customizers had made their mark on dry lakes and racetracks.

167
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:13,520
But a younger generation would soon put the hot in a hot ride by taking it to the streets.

168
00:13:16,420 --> 00:13:23,580
NASCAR legend and former moonshine runner Junior Johnson once served 11 months in prison after a raid on his family's still.

169
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:26,660
Custom cars will return on Modern Marvels.

170
00:13:26,661 --> 00:13:32,160
We now return to Custom Cars on Modern Marvels.

171
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:41,880
At a raceway near Bakersfield, California,

172
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:49,160
long rows of meticulously restored roadsters and customs strut their stuff at the annual Hot Rod Reunion.

173
00:13:49,940 --> 00:13:54,620
It's a scene that harkens back to an era when Hot Rods were an emblem of California teen culture.

174
00:13:54,621 --> 00:13:56,920
like surfboards and rock and roll,

175
00:13:57,700 --> 00:13:59,280
viewed with suspicion by parents

176
00:13:59,281 --> 00:14:01,280
and with envy by peers.

177
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:03,400
Those were simpler times,

178
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:05,600
less complicated, less nuanced.

179
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:08,480
You know, so you had a big snarling V8.

180
00:14:08,720 --> 00:14:10,600
You know, pretty much the world was your oyster.

181
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:19,220
Hot Rodding's golden age began shortly after World War II.

182
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:22,500
Production of new cars had been suspended during the war,

183
00:14:22,501 --> 00:14:26,020
And once it resumed, older models became cheap and plentiful.

184
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:32,500
A younger generation of hot rodders gradually developed a look that reflected the need for speed.

185
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:36,320
Hoods and fenders were stripped off, even for street driving.

186
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:43,700
While a chopped windshield and baloney-sliced air stacks above the carburetors all but screamed power.

187
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:48,360
Right here, we have basically the essence of a hot rod.

188
00:14:48,361 --> 00:14:53,300
It's a 32 Ford Roadster, original 32 frame, 32 Roadster body, steel body.

189
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:59,700
No amenities, no air conditioning, no power steering, no power brakes, just pure power.

190
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:06,280
In a lightweight body, stripped of anything non-necessary to make it go fast.

191
00:15:14,220 --> 00:15:15,580
There they are!

192
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:29,420
Hollywood's hot rod image of reckless abandon wasn't entirely contrived.

193
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:45,620
Unlike the Dry Lakes racers, Southern California's hot rodders did their racing on the street, with often disastrous results.

194
00:15:46,380 --> 00:15:48,860
There was a lot of stigma against hot rodders.

195
00:15:49,560 --> 00:15:53,360
Because of the street racing and the unsafe nature of the cars,

196
00:15:53,460 --> 00:15:55,940
they were just put together basically a body on a chassis,

197
00:15:56,060 --> 00:15:57,640
the lighter the better, with a big motor,

198
00:15:58,000 --> 00:15:59,320
not necessarily good on brakes,

199
00:15:59,420 --> 00:16:01,760
not necessarily a lot of them good on suspension or turning,

200
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:05,820
and they'd go out and race and crash and people would die.

201
00:16:11,420 --> 00:16:13,220
To help corral the problem,

202
00:16:13,221 --> 00:16:19,380
Hot Rod magazine editor Wally Parks formed the National Hot Rod Association in 1951

203
00:16:19,381 --> 00:16:22,620
to move races off the streets and into sanctioned areas.

204
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:25,240
The beginning of modern-day drag racing.

205
00:16:30,820 --> 00:16:37,720
There were so many drag strips you could just go out and partake on a Wednesday night or a Friday, Saturday, Sunday, wide open.

206
00:16:37,721 --> 00:16:43,380
and got almost everybody off the street as far as street racing was going on.

207
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:51,000
While hot rods aimed for speed, classic customs celebrated style.

208
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:55,700
These were full-bodied cars whose outlines were molded and streamlined

209
00:16:55,701 --> 00:16:58,060
to create a more aesthetically pleasing look.

210
00:16:59,580 --> 00:17:03,120
Early customizers painted their cars in dark, subtle hues.

211
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:07,560
They removed door handles and emblems and stripped away excess chrome.

212
00:17:07,720 --> 00:17:14,560
Fenders were extended to create long, graceful lines, while headlights were Frenched by molding them inward.

213
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:24,680
Replacing stock parts with higher-end grills, bumpers, and wheel covers bequeathed an exotic, uncertain identity to otherwise familiar Fords and Mercurys.

214
00:17:24,681 --> 00:17:27,720
They could take a Cadillac grille and put it in this Ford.

215
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:37,046
They could take the Cadillac wheel covers, take the big white wall tires that were on the fancy cars, put the Buick skirts over the back, and all of a sudden, what is

216
00:17:37,047 --> 00:17:37,747
this car?

217
00:17:37,748 --> 00:17:46,820
More radical alterations included channeling, in which the floor was removed so the body could be lowered over the frame.

218
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:49,380
Then the floor was reattached.

219
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:55,240
Channeling left less room inside the car, but brought the body closer to the ground.

220
00:17:56,180 --> 00:18:02,400
They had to be low, they had to be raked just right, you know, they had to look like great big bars of soap sliding down the street.

221
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:14,760
Magazines showcase the work of top California customizers, like Sam and George Barris.

222
00:18:15,320 --> 00:18:23,420
I was able to photograph as we were building cars, and I would put how-to, these are done, in the magazines.

223
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:29,860
So the guy back in Kokomo, Indiana, said, oh, I can now chop a top because I've seen the way Barris did it.

224
00:18:31,740 --> 00:18:35,440
But every work we chopped was different.

225
00:18:35,441 --> 00:18:38,080
We never made one or two exact.

226
00:18:39,060 --> 00:18:44,320
A 1951 Mercury customized by the Barris Brothers for its owner, Bob Hirohata,

227
00:18:44,620 --> 00:18:48,640
was driven from Los Angeles to Indianapolis to compete in a major car show.

228
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:52,240
When it took the top prize, a star was born.

229
00:18:52,680 --> 00:18:57,500
He came up with this, what at the time was a totally revolutionary color,

230
00:18:58,100 --> 00:19:02,060
which was to go away from these deep, dark colors to this bright pastel,

231
00:19:02,061 --> 00:19:06,220
seafoam green or ice green, whatever you want to call it.

232
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:17,480
Restored to its original state by its owner of 40 years, Jim McNeil,

233
00:19:17,980 --> 00:19:20,980
the Hirohata Merc may be the most famous custom ever made.

234
00:19:22,020 --> 00:19:24,880
For many, its flowing lines and exquisite detail

235
00:19:24,881 --> 00:19:27,840
mark the apex of the lean, clean custom style.

236
00:19:27,841 --> 00:19:33,840
The grill in this car is made of three 1951 Ford grills.

237
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:37,700
This would be the center section. It used to have bullets here.

238
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:40,960
They took the bullets out, moved these pieces in,

239
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:44,560
and then they took another section like this and made one here,

240
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:48,760
and another section from this end, and put it over here together,

241
00:19:48,761 --> 00:19:53,800
same on this side, and then handmade these plastic parking lights.

242
00:19:53,801 --> 00:20:00,040
so it was one of the first cars that actually took up the whole front with a nice wide floating type of grille.

243
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:06,860
There used to be a dip here that they removed and brought this line straight back to this point

244
00:20:06,861 --> 00:20:12,960
and then they actually split the fender here and brought this side out

245
00:20:12,961 --> 00:20:17,960
where a piece of metal was welded in here all the way back.

246
00:20:17,961 --> 00:20:21,840
these are 52 Chevy teeth right here

247
00:20:21,841 --> 00:20:24,120
out of a grill

248
00:20:24,121 --> 00:20:26,640
and then they made the skirts

249
00:20:26,641 --> 00:20:29,480
that are flush mounted with the fender

250
00:20:29,481 --> 00:20:32,480
and these are 52 Lincoln taillights

251
00:20:32,481 --> 00:20:35,780
and they made a nice little roll around there

252
00:20:35,781 --> 00:20:38,220
and smoothed it all in around there

253
00:20:38,221 --> 00:20:40,080
and cut the bumper so this fit

254
00:20:40,081 --> 00:20:43,080
and then they ran the exhaust tips

255
00:20:43,081 --> 00:20:44,220
right out through the bumper

256
00:20:44,221 --> 00:20:47,060
which was a really custom touch

257
00:20:47,061 --> 00:20:50,680
You know, the pipes hung down and you would scrape them anyway.

258
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:56,360
So that was, you know, and that was something that really caught on.

259
00:20:57,060 --> 00:21:02,340
Inside the car, green and white tuck-and-roll pleats are augmented by handcrafted dash knobs

260
00:21:02,341 --> 00:21:05,940
and pinstripes by the celebrated custom artist Von Dutch.

261
00:21:07,560 --> 00:21:09,260
But how do you get into this car?

262
00:21:10,980 --> 00:21:14,800
Pressing a hidden button along the chrome strip activates a solenoid,

263
00:21:14,801 --> 00:21:17,580
a kind of triggering device inside the door panel.

264
00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:20,080
The solenoid's plunger pulls the latch.

265
00:21:20,940 --> 00:21:22,420
The trunk opens the same way.

266
00:21:23,240 --> 00:21:26,860
And you can see when you push the button, this magnet pulls in,

267
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:30,020
and it pulls this trunk latch open, and that's what opens the trunk.

268
00:21:30,420 --> 00:21:33,560
They drive into a drive-in, and they get out, close the door,

269
00:21:33,820 --> 00:21:37,080
and, you know, the Joe family man would look over and go,

270
00:21:37,440 --> 00:21:38,880
how are you going to get back in that thing?

271
00:21:38,881 --> 00:21:50,200
The Hirohata Merc's vibrant two-tone paint also signaled a transition to an era of more visually striking candy colors.

272
00:21:50,960 --> 00:22:00,460
These were bright toners applied over a base of gold or silver paint, then covered with up to 40 coats of clear lacquer and hand-rubbed to a blending sheen.

273
00:22:01,340 --> 00:22:04,700
Gene Winfield developed a technique of blending candies with pearls.

274
00:22:05,660 --> 00:22:11,560
Early pearls were finely ground seashells mixed with lacquer and sprayed over the toner to create a subtle gleam.

275
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:16,560
I would paint a car white and put the gold pearl over the white.

276
00:22:17,300 --> 00:22:22,700
And in the sun, in the highlighted areas, the gold would just jump out at you.

277
00:22:23,100 --> 00:22:30,520
But then on the downside, where you'd look down across the shadowed side, it had a very slight blue cast.

278
00:22:30,521 --> 00:22:34,980
As the 50s progressed, customs grew even more ornate.

279
00:22:35,700 --> 00:22:39,540
Candies and pearls gave way to panel paint, scallops, and flames.

280
00:22:43,140 --> 00:22:48,120
Imaginative custom designs like Ed Big Daddy Roth's cartoonish fiberglass bodies,

281
00:22:49,060 --> 00:22:51,260
Daryl Starbird's futuristic bubble tops,

282
00:22:51,940 --> 00:22:56,060
and George Barris' Batmobile, featured in the popular 60s TV show,

283
00:22:56,480 --> 00:22:58,620
turned their creators into icons.

284
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:07,700
For a glorious decade, customs and hot rods flourished as antidotes to Detroit's relatively stodgy style.

285
00:23:10,060 --> 00:23:14,820
But by the late 50s, major manufacturers were aping the streamlined customs look.

286
00:23:16,840 --> 00:23:18,920
Styling verges on the spectacular.

287
00:23:19,300 --> 00:23:24,380
From air-scooped grills to dramatically sweeping tail fins that dominate most of the 57 models,

288
00:23:24,720 --> 00:23:27,320
the cars are longer and lower than ever this year, too.

289
00:23:30,520 --> 00:23:37,120
A few years later, Detroit began producing lines of muscle cars and pony cars

290
00:23:37,121 --> 00:23:39,860
that combined sporty looks with hot rod horsepower.

291
00:23:41,640 --> 00:23:43,400
Mainstream imitations were flattering,

292
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:46,680
but for hot rodders and customizers, their effects were devastating.

293
00:23:47,020 --> 00:23:51,740
Now a customer can walk into the dealership and buy a muscle car for maybe $4,000 or $5,000

294
00:23:51,741 --> 00:23:57,960
and instead of spending the same money on a car that they already paid $3,000 or $4,000 for.

295
00:23:57,961 --> 00:24:02,120
So Detroit really just totally ruined the custom business.

296
00:24:03,880 --> 00:24:06,900
Hot rods and customs pretty much disappeared from the streets.

297
00:24:09,020 --> 00:24:12,540
And they stayed gone for a couple of decades.

298
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:18,640
But even as classic customs faded from view,

299
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:23,280
an emerging car culture would ride lower and slower than ever.

300
00:24:24,920 --> 00:24:27,640
To create the original TV Batmobile,

301
00:24:27,960 --> 00:24:33,960
George Barris customized a 1955 Lincoln Concept car called the Futuro.

302
00:24:34,460 --> 00:24:37,960
Custom cars will return on Modern Marvels.

303
00:24:39,960 --> 00:24:43,960
We now return to Custom cars on Modern Marvels.

304
00:24:57,960 --> 00:25:09,980
East of Los Angeles on a Sunday afternoon, a cavalcade of shimmering, metal-flaked, pinstriped cruisers practice a unique style of hop and roll.

305
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:19,280
For these custom cars, it's all part of a weekend ritual that's been going on for decades, the dance of the lowriders.

306
00:25:19,800 --> 00:25:23,200
Lowriding, to me, is a flashy car.

307
00:25:24,360 --> 00:25:26,120
But it's more than that, I would say.

308
00:25:26,121 --> 00:25:28,420
low riding is a lifestyle.

309
00:25:29,200 --> 00:25:35,260
I mean, just having a vehicle to portray my own individuality.

310
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:40,020
Low riding came into its own during the 1960s,

311
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:43,140
even as other forms of customizing were going out of style.

312
00:25:43,860 --> 00:25:46,120
Its roots can be traced to the 1940s,

313
00:25:46,380 --> 00:25:49,920
when fashion-minded zoot suiters gave their cars a stylized look

314
00:25:49,921 --> 00:25:51,680
by pushing them close to the ground,

315
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:54,480
sometimes by piling sandbags in the trunk.

316
00:25:54,481 --> 00:26:00,720
But as lowriding developed in Southern California's black and Latino communities, it also became

317
00:26:00,721 --> 00:26:02,480
part of a larger social movement.

318
00:26:02,481 --> 00:26:07,160
There was this cultural renaissance in black and brown neighborhoods, you know, in the

319
00:26:07,161 --> 00:26:10,920
40s and 50s and 60s, where it was music, it was cars.

320
00:26:10,921 --> 00:26:18,500
It was about creating, I think, a very Los Angeles youth cultural identity.

321
00:26:18,501 --> 00:26:24,360
Like customs, the lowrider look evolved from muted colors, often gray or black primer paint,

322
00:26:24,361 --> 00:26:28,200
more brilliant candies, pearls, and metal flakes.

323
00:26:28,201 --> 00:26:31,580
Early metal flake paints consisted of tiny aluminum squares

324
00:26:31,581 --> 00:26:34,800
or shaved chrome, which were mixed with clear lacquer,

325
00:26:34,801 --> 00:26:38,940
sprayed over the base, and then covered with more lacquer

326
00:26:38,941 --> 00:26:41,760
to overlie the metal tips before sanding and buffing.

327
00:26:44,300 --> 00:26:48,100
We'll just spray it on there, and that thing will twinkle.

328
00:26:48,101 --> 00:26:50,480
It was just awesome.

329
00:26:50,481 --> 00:26:54,000
And you can see that deepness, that rich,

330
00:26:54,001 --> 00:26:58,280
where you can, if it's a candy apple red paint job, you just want to bite it.

331
00:26:59,220 --> 00:27:02,140
Though metal flake remains popular among lowriders,

332
00:27:02,820 --> 00:27:07,780
contemporary cars also sport a combination of abstract pinstriping and lines of gold leaf.

333
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:15,160
Interiors have evolved from velvet and velour to leather, with ostrich and even alligator trim.

334
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:18,240
Yeah, we spare no expense on the ostrich.

335
00:27:18,241 --> 00:27:19,520
We used ostrich on the visor.

336
00:27:20,460 --> 00:27:21,560
We used ostrich on the piping.

337
00:27:21,700 --> 00:27:22,980
Most people would have just ditched it.

338
00:27:22,981 --> 00:27:27,800
But we said, no, we've got to follow the scheme all the way around with ostrich all the way through.

339
00:27:28,840 --> 00:27:32,260
And that's it. We just try to make it plain and simple, make it look good.

340
00:27:37,280 --> 00:27:44,340
Hand-painted murals display mythic imagery, religious icons, and celebrations of the female form.

341
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:49,620
Art became part of a way to inspire the community to take pride in their culture,

342
00:27:49,621 --> 00:27:55,500
to celebrate their culture, and to use art as a way to tell a story.

343
00:27:56,040 --> 00:27:59,080
And I think unlike murals that are on walls,

344
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:03,680
I think that the murals on cars allow you to take it out of the boundaries of the radio.

345
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:09,880
Of course, the primary aim of a lowrider is to ride low, very low.

346
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:13,680
Early techniques included installing smaller tires,

347
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:15,820
which brought the frame closer to the ground.

348
00:28:15,821 --> 00:28:20,640
Coil suspensions were torched with several coils removed to shorten their

349
00:28:20,641 --> 00:28:25,980
reach. For leaf spring suspensions, leaves were rearranged to flatten the

350
00:28:25,981 --> 00:28:30,300
curve, then topped with lowering blocks to pull the chassis closer to the wheels,

351
00:28:30,301 --> 00:28:35,660
making the car lower still. A magnesium plate attached to the undercarriage

352
00:28:35,661 --> 00:28:40,520
provided the payoff, creating colorful sparks when a lowrider literally hit the

353
00:28:40,521 --> 00:28:42,780
highway.

354
00:28:45,820 --> 00:28:49,620
But riding low also sparked trouble with the law.

355
00:28:50,620 --> 00:28:59,540
To try and clear the streets of these vehicles, a 1959 California statute prohibited any part of a car from dipping below the bottom of the wheel rim.

356
00:29:01,880 --> 00:29:11,908
Low riders responded by developing battery-powered hydraulic systems, which could raise or drop a suspension while a car was in motion, navigating it over rough roads

357
00:29:11,909 --> 00:29:13,600
and past police inspections.

358
00:29:13,601 --> 00:29:18,920
The evolution of the hopping kind of came from adding the batteries,

359
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:23,300
the vehicle eventually coming up off the ground because it was coming up so fast.

360
00:29:23,960 --> 00:29:28,900
One guy just got the idea, hey, let me see what I can do and let's make this thing bounce.

361
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:39,520
At Homie's Hydraulics, east of Los Angeles,

362
00:29:39,521 --> 00:29:44,300
A customized suspension system begins with the installation of metal frames,

363
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:47,600
which are welded directly to the chassis from inside the trunk.

364
00:29:50,220 --> 00:29:55,720
Coil springs are trimmed to lower their height, then fitted to hydraulic cylinders or strokes.

365
00:29:56,920 --> 00:30:00,380
Here we have your actual stroke. This is a 10-inch stroke.

366
00:30:01,020 --> 00:30:03,900
We've got your donut right here, and we've got a cup.

367
00:30:04,680 --> 00:30:09,040
This cup actually sits inside the actual coil right here.

368
00:30:09,520 --> 00:30:14,520
And this little piece gets welded onto the trailing arm so it doesn't slip around

369
00:30:14,521 --> 00:30:16,520
and everything's locked into place like that.

370
00:30:17,520 --> 00:30:19,520
It's going to come up in here.

371
00:30:23,520 --> 00:30:25,520
And sit in there just like that.

372
00:30:29,520 --> 00:30:34,520
And then when that oil gets pumped into the hose, there's a shaft in here.

373
00:30:34,521 --> 00:30:37,520
It's going to extend and this is going to hit all the way to the top of the frame,

374
00:30:37,521 --> 00:30:40,647
frame compressing the coil making the car go up.

375
00:30:40,648 --> 00:30:43,840
The cylinders are connected by hoses to motorized

376
00:30:43,841 --> 00:30:49,600
hydraulic pumps similar to those used in the gate lifts of delivery trucks and filled with 30 weight

377
00:30:49,601 --> 00:30:50,301
motor oil.

378
00:30:50,302 --> 00:30:55,680
The pumps can both propel and retract fluid from the cylinders by flipping switches

379
00:30:55,681 --> 00:30:57,838
up or down on the instrument panel.

380
00:30:57,839 --> 00:31:01,600
You know you hit the front switch down the car is going to go

381
00:31:01,601 --> 00:31:04,191
down. You hit it up, it's gonna go up.

382
00:31:04,192 --> 00:31:07,600
Same thing with the rear and the two back corners.

383
00:31:07,601 --> 00:31:12,098
The more potent the battery power, the higher the hop.

384
00:31:12,099 --> 00:31:15,680
This car is outfitted with eight batteries,

385
00:31:15,681 --> 00:31:17,621
two pumps, and four switches.

386
00:31:17,622 --> 00:31:22,240
It's not too much, it's not too little, so it's just right. You know,

387
00:31:22,241 --> 00:31:26,160
someone comes up, pulls on you, starts hopping, and you don't want to just sit there and let them

388
00:31:26,161 --> 00:31:31,000
hop on you so you answer right back and that's just enough.

389
00:31:31,001 --> 00:31:36,980
Hydraulic setups are also designed to compete for points at car shows based on visual appeal.

390
00:31:36,981 --> 00:31:41,460
Part of an overall display which includes heavily chromed engines and undercarriages

391
00:31:41,461 --> 00:31:44,920
and pin striping under the hood.

392
00:31:44,921 --> 00:31:50,240
Things that would have been thought out of the norm for a basic low rider is now a standard

393
00:31:50,241 --> 00:31:51,920
and creates points.

394
00:31:56,160 --> 00:32:03,160
Anti-cruising laws have made it more difficult for lowriders to strut their stuff beyond the bounds of officially sanctioned shows.

395
00:32:03,161 --> 00:32:12,160
But the scene still thrives in more informal settings, where social rituals, and even the cars themselves, link generations of families and friends.

396
00:32:12,161 --> 00:32:17,160
What's up, man? How you doing?

397
00:32:17,161 --> 00:32:20,920
Car guys are family guys.

398
00:32:21,020 --> 00:32:22,580
They want to take their children out.

399
00:32:23,200 --> 00:32:28,820
They want to take their nephews, cousins, aunts, grandfathers and grandmothers.

400
00:32:29,380 --> 00:32:30,260
It's a family thing.

401
00:32:31,140 --> 00:32:32,320
It's for the love of it.

402
00:32:32,460 --> 00:32:35,480
It's in the blood, and that's the way of life.

403
00:32:35,860 --> 00:32:37,100
That's our way of life.

404
00:32:38,160 --> 00:32:41,080
Lowriders made Big and Slow the way to go.

405
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:46,740
But a new generation has given custom cars the strangest spin of all.

406
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:53,820
The record for a lowrider hop exceeds 10 feet.

407
00:32:55,080 --> 00:32:57,860
Custom cars will return on Modern Marvels.

408
00:32:59,980 --> 00:33:03,660
We now return to Custom Cars on Modern Marvels.

409
00:33:13,300 --> 00:33:16,740
It's a strangely graceful form of pageantry on wheels.

410
00:33:17,160 --> 00:33:25,500
A competition in which cars are deliberately oversteered to slide and spin through a series of sharp turns.

411
00:33:26,820 --> 00:33:31,700
Instead of racing toward a finish line, contestants are judged on execution and style.

412
00:33:32,820 --> 00:33:40,760
It's a bunch of nutters driving on the absolute edge of crashing against each other, inches apart.

413
00:33:41,500 --> 00:33:45,340
It's really a style thing, you know, how far can you push it?

414
00:33:47,160 --> 00:33:54,160
Started in Japan, drifting is caught on with a generation of customizers

415
00:33:54,161 --> 00:33:58,600
who introduced a new kind of custom car in Southern California in the 1980s,

416
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:00,100
the import tuner.

417
00:34:04,500 --> 00:34:09,780
Like the early hot rodders, the tuner crowd made their mark by embracing cheap hand-me-downs,

418
00:34:10,420 --> 00:34:14,260
in this case, inexpensive Japanese economy cars like the Honda Civic,

419
00:34:14,860 --> 00:34:16,500
and turning them into road warriors.

420
00:34:17,160 --> 00:34:26,840
These were cars that Asian American families bought for their kids to go to school.

421
00:34:27,340 --> 00:34:28,540
And of course you've got a Honda Civic.

422
00:34:28,720 --> 00:34:31,240
Well, you don't want it to be looking like everybody else's Honda Civic.

423
00:34:32,440 --> 00:34:36,060
So you're going to slam it, you're going to put some rims on it, you're going to put a spoiler on it,

424
00:34:36,120 --> 00:34:37,260
you're going to paint it a different color.

425
00:34:37,560 --> 00:34:38,880
Next minute you're going to race it.

426
00:34:38,881 --> 00:34:48,100
Until this new breed came along, customizing seemed stuck in time.

427
00:34:49,180 --> 00:34:55,140
The 70s gas crisis, coupled with tougher emission standards and later computerized engine control,

428
00:34:55,680 --> 00:34:58,660
dissuaded many hot rodders from working on contemporary cars.

429
00:35:00,820 --> 00:35:06,000
But a generation raised on the Internet could plug software programs into their car's computer ports

430
00:35:06,001 --> 00:35:08,520
and changed the factory engine specifications,

431
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:12,040
which were often set well below the car's performance capabilities.

432
00:35:14,680 --> 00:35:19,660
The engine management programs that are used to control engines

433
00:35:19,661 --> 00:35:25,240
could be hacked and the parameters change to give you more performance.

434
00:35:26,180 --> 00:35:30,840
And because engine durability had been improved so much

435
00:35:30,841 --> 00:35:34,320
in the 20 years between, say, 1970 and 1990,

436
00:35:34,321 --> 00:35:40,320
That made the engines more tolerant of being hot-rodded.

437
00:35:43,320 --> 00:35:47,320
Import tuners can also be hot-rodded in more traditional ways.

438
00:35:48,320 --> 00:35:55,320
Installing a turbocharger, an exhaust-driven pump that forces air into the combustion chamber at higher pressure, helps increase horsepower.

439
00:35:55,321 --> 00:35:56,123
horsepower.

440
00:35:56,124 --> 00:36:01,820
Another way to add power is to inject nitrous oxide into the air fuel mixture,

441
00:36:02,140 --> 00:36:05,380
which enriches the oxygen content in the cylinders as they're fired.

442
00:36:06,300 --> 00:36:10,460
They put turbochargers on them. They put different cylinder heads on them. They put

443
00:36:10,461 --> 00:36:11,900
nitrous oxide on them.

444
00:36:11,901 --> 00:36:15,960
And suddenly, a whole industry grew up around that little car.

445
00:36:17,760 --> 00:36:23,080
As the popularity of import tuners grows, Japanese and American manufacturers have

446
00:36:23,081 --> 00:36:27,880
stepped up their lines of aftermarket engine parts, which can be ordered and installed under warranty.

447
00:36:28,880 --> 00:36:33,820
It's a form of car customizing that's gradually changing the attitude among owners from DIY,

448
00:36:34,480 --> 00:36:37,560
do-it-yourself, to DIFM, do-it-for-me.

449
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:44,880
So now you can pull your car into a garage, have somebody bolt a turbocharger on it,

450
00:36:44,940 --> 00:36:48,780
bigger turbocharger, cold air induction, various other things.

451
00:36:48,781 --> 00:36:53,540
They can do a lot to the top end of the motor and get more performance.

452
00:36:55,020 --> 00:37:02,360
At Pomona, California's SoCal Speed Shop, a late model Chevrolet Cobalt, equipped with a standard Ecotec engine,

453
00:37:02,700 --> 00:37:10,760
has been customized so a driver can boost its turbocharger settings at the touch of a button and push it to speeds up to 243 miles per hour.

454
00:37:11,300 --> 00:37:17,480
This is a Chevrolet Cobalt, and it's a steel-bodied car, pretty much like the production car you can buy from a dealer.

455
00:37:17,481 --> 00:37:23,420
basically what we've done working with Roush Industries is put this seven point roll cage in

456
00:37:23,421 --> 00:37:28,820
there hands device to protect your head and it has this wonderful little gadget on the steering

457
00:37:28,821 --> 00:37:36,820
wheel where you can increase the boost 5 10 15 20 25 30 35 40 45 pounds of boost it's just like a

458
00:37:36,821 --> 00:37:45,920
video game import tuners are going for style points as well for many that means neon lights

459
00:37:45,921 --> 00:37:48,080
and high-end video and sound systems.

460
00:37:50,140 --> 00:37:53,100
They're often on display at multimedia events

461
00:37:53,101 --> 00:37:55,700
that feature DJs, dancing,

462
00:37:56,040 --> 00:37:58,480
and a wide array of up-to-date models.

463
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:03,940
They're not your father's auto shows.

464
00:38:04,440 --> 00:38:06,540
You go to a car show and some of the stuff

465
00:38:06,541 --> 00:38:09,240
they manage to do with them is just mad, really mad.

466
00:38:09,860 --> 00:38:12,220
These days, a lot of the events are trying to encompass car shows,

467
00:38:12,500 --> 00:38:15,720
racing, drifting, models, all of it.

468
00:38:15,721 --> 00:38:24,620
This thing is all about aesthetics, whether it's aesthetics of the car sitting still or whether it's aesthetics of the car in action, smoke in the tire.

469
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:31,120
Drag racing, I mean, it's, you know, it's a very, very visual motorsport, more so than maybe anything else,

470
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:37,500
because it's all about, you know, ground-shaking power and smoke and smells and, you know, it's what makes it exciting.

471
00:38:38,020 --> 00:38:40,900
They're doing everything the hot rodders and the customizers did.

472
00:38:40,901 --> 00:38:45,120
It's just that they have a different look, you know, but it's still the same game.

473
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:54,520
But even as import tuners define the state of the art, older generations of customizers are giving vintage rides a modern makeover.

474
00:38:57,040 --> 00:39:05,260
In 2005, a customized Honda Civic exceeded 195 miles per hour on the Bonneville salt flats.

475
00:39:06,860 --> 00:39:09,740
Custom cars will return on Modern Marvels.

476
00:39:09,741 --> 00:39:16,740
We now return to Custom Cars on Modern Marvels.

477
00:39:16,741 --> 00:39:30,741
Once expressions of separate subcultures, hot rods, customs, low riders, and import tuners

478
00:39:32,740 --> 00:39:37,740
have adapted each other's ideas and innovations to become ever more homogenous.

479
00:39:37,741 --> 00:39:39,740
An American melting pot on wheels.

480
00:39:40,800 --> 00:39:49,920
One of the lessons we can take out of these things is how much creative energy and artistic enterprise there are in just regular people.

481
00:39:51,060 --> 00:39:54,780
It's extraordinary when you look back and look at some of these cars and say,

482
00:39:54,860 --> 00:40:00,880
these guys were, you know, they were factory workers and machinists and clerks.

483
00:40:00,881 --> 00:40:07,540
And in a way, it kind of reminds us of the innate genius in all of us.

484
00:40:07,740 --> 00:40:10,240
and who knows where it will go next.

485
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:16,460
This is the GTO Roadster, retro-backed from the 60s.

486
00:40:16,700 --> 00:40:21,220
How would you like to drive a Roadster GTO in 2005 and 2006?

487
00:40:21,500 --> 00:40:24,660
This will give you an idea of what we do here in California.

488
00:40:27,060 --> 00:40:29,900
For legendary customizer George Barris,

489
00:40:30,260 --> 00:40:33,440
the future of custom cars inevitably draws from its past.

490
00:40:33,441 --> 00:40:41,800
His restyled Pontiac GTO combines the old-fashioned power of a muscle car with the modern accoutrements of an import tuner,

491
00:40:42,320 --> 00:40:49,040
such as vertically hinged doors, pairs of 20 and 22-inch wheels, and cameras installed inside the taillights,

492
00:40:49,420 --> 00:40:52,800
which project views through video monitors mounted on the dash.

493
00:40:53,120 --> 00:40:55,140
I took the side mirrors and I threw them away.

494
00:40:55,800 --> 00:40:57,393
And all I do is look at my dash now.

495
00:40:57,394 --> 00:41:00,180
I've got two monitors I can see, 45 degrees anywhere around me.

496
00:41:00,181 --> 00:41:09,660
You'll find that John Coupoli, whether he's a car guy or not, is going to definitely look at this GTO and enjoy it.

497
00:41:11,040 --> 00:41:13,420
Outfitting customs has become big business.

498
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:16,540
The garage where Vic Edelbrock Sr.

499
00:41:16,541 --> 00:41:21,260
began designing manifolds has evolved into a sprawling manufacturing plant.

500
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:27,260
My father used to get an idea, and the pattern maker would kind of draw it out on a piece of wood.

501
00:41:27,261 --> 00:41:31,740
and he'd say I want so much area under the carburetor, and I want so much taper,

502
00:41:32,240 --> 00:41:37,400
and that today we do it much, much better because we've got the equipment to do it with.

503
00:41:38,300 --> 00:41:42,500
These days, Edelbrock makes more types of aftermarket parts than any other company.

504
00:41:44,440 --> 00:41:50,340
Its signature products, manifolds and cylinder heads, are still crafted entirely in-house.

505
00:41:50,341 --> 00:41:52,600
from computerized designs

506
00:41:52,601 --> 00:41:55,300
to casting and machining

507
00:41:55,301 --> 00:41:58,320
to packaging for final sale.

508
00:41:59,480 --> 00:42:00,940
And when we're through,

509
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:02,800
it's strictly take out of the box

510
00:42:02,801 --> 00:42:04,140
and put it on the engine.

511
00:42:04,420 --> 00:42:05,660
This is the key to the program.

512
00:42:08,140 --> 00:42:10,780
The demand for old school hot rods and customs

513
00:42:10,781 --> 00:42:11,840
is surging as well.

514
00:42:12,560 --> 00:42:14,900
Now it's possible to either build a car,

515
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:15,700
mail order,

516
00:42:15,880 --> 00:42:17,340
or to go to a shop like ours

517
00:42:17,341 --> 00:42:18,760
and have a car either part built

518
00:42:18,761 --> 00:42:19,760
or completely built.

519
00:42:19,761 --> 00:42:24,340
So it's real easy to have something that looks old, but that works in a modern way.

520
00:42:25,860 --> 00:42:28,020
Brian Brennan has done just that.

521
00:42:28,900 --> 00:42:36,600
His street rod, a 1932 Model A body perched on a 29 frame, melds classic style with contemporary performance.

522
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:43,740
The grille shell is a 1932 Ford with a 32 Ford inset.

523
00:42:44,220 --> 00:42:49,140
The hood is a three-piece hood, and it's aluminum, and in effect it's a custom hood.

524
00:42:49,700 --> 00:42:55,540
The windshield frame is reminiscent of a Model A, but here again, it's not Model A.

525
00:42:55,580 --> 00:42:57,040
It's also a custom piece.

526
00:42:58,500 --> 00:43:01,280
The front suspension, what's holding in place?

527
00:43:01,281 --> 00:43:06,040
Well, those are hairpins that are from SoCal here in Pomona, California.

528
00:43:06,600 --> 00:43:09,560
And the wheels are reminiscent of early race car wheels.

529
00:43:09,960 --> 00:43:12,980
The interior, very traditional in its style.

530
00:43:13,140 --> 00:43:14,500
Very plain, very simple.

531
00:43:15,140 --> 00:43:19,020
It's a simple pleat, and it's done in black leather.

532
00:43:19,140 --> 00:43:20,980
It's a roadster, all right?

533
00:43:21,260 --> 00:43:23,680
And in my case, I've chosen not to have a top.

534
00:43:24,240 --> 00:43:27,380
Some people think I'm crazy to want to drive all the time without a top.

535
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:31,240
I like to think that I get to see things they don't get to see.

536
00:43:37,940 --> 00:43:41,140
In this overwhelmingly male-driven automotive culture,

537
00:43:41,820 --> 00:43:44,220
custom cars may be the ultimate boys' toys,

538
00:43:44,221 --> 00:43:48,840
both the symbol and the object of an age-old romantic quest.

539
00:43:49,140 --> 00:43:58,480
On one side you can see that part of customizing your car hopefully will translate into you attracting a beautiful woman.

540
00:43:59,060 --> 00:44:04,500
And the other side is that the car does take on these elements of a woman.

541
00:44:06,420 --> 00:44:08,940
Are cars male or female?

542
00:44:10,660 --> 00:44:16,780
It depends on the owner and it also depends on the emotional state of the owner.

543
00:44:19,140 --> 00:44:24,240
The one thing about automobiles is that, you know, they rarely say no.

